DURATION:PT1H
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
IMAGE;VALUE=URI;DISPLAY=BADGE;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://inprinthouston.org
 /wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Andrea-Bajani-and-Claire-Vaye-Watkins-closeup-
 1-for-website.jpg
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://inprinthouston.org/wp-content/uploads/202
 6/07/Andrea-Bajani-and-Claire-Vaye-Watkins-closeup-1-for-website.jpg
UID:bPsah6uBCGHOWQA0EE9Qa
SUMMARY:Inprint Andrea Bajani & Claire Vaye Watkins Reading
DTSTAMP:20260724T105422Z
DTSTART:20260824T193000Z
DESCRIPTION:General admission tickets will go on sale on Monday\, August 3\
	, 2026 at 12 pm. \nAndrea Bajani and Claire Vaye Watkins will read from th
	eir new novels\, respectively\, The Anniversary (translated by Geoffrey Br
	ock) and Yellow Pine\, followed by an on-stage conversation led by profess
	or and National Book Critics Circle Award finalist Lacy M. Johnson. The ev
	ening will conclude with a book sale and signing. The event is presented a
	s part of the 2026/2027 Inprint Margarett Root Brown Reading Series.  \nAn
	drea Bajani is one of the most recognized and lauded authors of contempora
	ry Italian and European literature. His many novels include If You Kept a 
	Record of Sins\, called “astonishing” by The New York Times\, and Every Pr
	omise\, which won the Bagutta Prize\, the oldest Italian literary prize. A
	 self-described existential writer\, he often examines the personal\, phil
	osophical\, and political\, while confronting the unanswerable. A professo
	r and writer-in-residence at Rice University\, Bajani joins us with his la
	test novel\, The Anniversary\, which won the 2025 Strega Prize\, one of Eu
	rope’s major literary awards. Currently being translated into more than 25
	 languages\, The Anniversary is a subtle yet heartrending portrait of an a
	busive marriage in patriarchal Italy and a son’s liberating decision to cu
	t contact with his parents. The Times Literary Supplement writes\, “Bajani
	’s prose is sharp and brims with psychological insights… Perhaps its most 
	salient feature—in deft translation by Geoffrey Brock—is its skill at depi
	cting alienation… Like Albert Camus\, Bajani is a poet of estrangement.” \
	nClaire Vaye Watkins has been called “the most riveting voice of the unsal
	vageable West” (Los Angeles Times). Her debut novel Gold Fame Citrus\, ins
	pired by the Californian drought and by the lives of outcasts in the deser
	t\, blends climate fiction with environmental history. Born in Bishop\, Ca
	lifornia and raised in the Mojave Desert on the Amargosa River\, Watkins i
	s also the author of the novel I Love You But I’ve Chosen Darkness and the
	 short story collection Battleborn\, winner of the Story Prize\, the Dylan
	 Thomas Prize\, and the New York Public Library’s Young Lions Fiction Awar
	d. A National Book Foundation “5 Under 35” honoree and a Guggenheim Fellow
	\, Watkins comes to Houston with her newest work Yellow Pine\, described b
	y author Megha Majumdar as “an exhilarating novel about a woman in the Moj
	ave desert who wants to be a mother one more time\, wants to love freely a
	nd arrange her life freely\, wants to live outside the maze of money and a
	ds and being sold to\, a novel with huge and electric convictions.” \nLacy
	 M. Johnson is a Houston-based professor\, curator\, and activist\, and is
	 author of The Reckonings and The Other Side — both National Book Critics 
	Circle Award finalists — and Trespasses. She is editor\, with the designer
	 Cheryl Beckett\, of More City Than Water: A Houston Flood Atlas. She teac
	hes creative nonfiction at Rice University\, where she is the Director of 
	the MFA in Creative Writing Program\, and is also the Founding Director of
	 the Houston Flood Museum. \n\n\nhttps://inprinthouston.org/event/inprint-
	andrea-bajani-claire-vaye-watkins-reading/\n\nFrom Inprint Houston
URL:https://inprinthouston.org/event/inprint-andrea-bajani-claire-vaye-watk
	ins-reading/
LOCATION:Congregation Emanu El\, 1500 Sunset Boulevard\, Houston\, TX\, 770
	05
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:inprint-houston
CATEGORIES:Inprint Houston
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT2H
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
IMAGE;VALUE=URI;DISPLAY=BADGE;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://inprinthouston.org
 /wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Photo_RexOgle%C2%A9DustinWooten-for-web.jpg
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://inprinthouston.org/wp-content/uploads/202
 6/06/Photo_RexOgle%C2%A9DustinWooten-for-web.jpg
UID:5rWqlQBTgZp2c7S1KcZac
SUMMARY:Inprint Cool Brains! Series presents Rex Ogle
DTSTAMP:20260724T105422Z
DTSTART:20260913T150000Z
DESCRIPTION:\nFree tickets available soon \n \n\n\nFree and open to the pub
	lic\nFree copies of one of Rex Ogle’s newest books for the first 100 famil
	ies\nPresentation and Q&A by the author\nMeet the author followed by a boo
	k sale and signing\nBus scholarships available for student groups – full s
	cholarships for public schools\, partial scholarships for private schools 
	upon availability. For information\, email info@inprint.org\n\nDiscounted 
	books available through Brazos Bookstore\n\n\nAnd more!\n\n\nThe 2026/2027
	 Inprint Cool Brains! Series proudly presents award-winning author Rex Ogl
	e\, sharing his new books Fruitcake\, a graphic novel about surviving midd
	le school and finding the courage to be yourself\, and Absent\, a middle g
	rade novel about three boys who skip school\, told in verse\, prose\, and 
	comics. \nRex Ogle is the author of more than 100 books for kids\, includi
	ng comics\, graphic novels\, and memoirs—most notably Free Lunch (winner o
	f the ALA/YALSA award for Excellence in Nonfiction)\, Abuela Don’t Forget 
	Me (ALA/YALSA award for Excellence in Nonfiction finalist)\, Road Home (a 
	Micheal Printz Honor and Stonewall Honor book)\, and middle grade graphic 
	novels Four Eyes and Pizza Face. Under his pseudonym Rey Terciero\, he has
	 also reimagined classics as modern and diverse graphic novels\, including
	 Meg\, Jo\, Beth\, & Amy\, Northranger (nominated for both Harvey and GLAA
	D Media Awards)\, and Dan in Green Gables (nominated for a GLAAD Media Awa
	rd). Born and raised in Texas\, Rex now lives in Los Angeles where he writ
	es every day—when he’s not reading X-Men\, gaming with friends\, or chasin
	g his dog\, Toby\, trying to score a hug. \nDescribed as “funny\, sad\, go
	ofy and poignant\; a winner” (Kirkus starred review)\, Fruitcake is the th
	ird and final installment of Ogle’s highly popular Four Eyes graphic novel
	 series\, which tackles relatable middle school themes like bullying\, pub
	erty\, building self-confidence\, and navigating friendships. Eighth grade
	 isn’t off to a great start for Rex. Everyone seems to be coupling up\, an
	d he’s starting to feel like an outsider—until he meets Charlotte. She’s f
	earless\, smart\, pretty\, and\, best of all\, she likes him back. But as 
	wonderful as Charlotte is\, Rex can’t stop thinking about someone else: Dr
	ew\, Rex’s childhood best friend. Suddenly\, Rex is navigating feelings he
	 never expected. With strong opinions swirling at home and church\, Rex be
	gins to question his own worth and what his feelings truly mean. Featuring
	 Dave Valeza’s vibrant\, colorful\, and engaging illustrations\, Fruitcake
	 is a heartfelt story of laughter and fear\, crushes and coming out\, and 
	the courage it takes to embrace who you are. \nWith illustrations by LJ-Ba
	ptiste\, Absent explores school absenteeism in a compelling\, entertaining
	\, and utterly distinct middle grade novel. With a dad in jail and a lack 
	of teachers interested in helping him learn\, Manny doesn’t see the point 
	in anything besides skateboarding and writing lyrics. Calvin has so many r
	esponsibilities to his father and little brothers that when his bike is st
	olen\, he can’t make it to school on time. And while Liam is studious and 
	wants to become a doctor\, he is tormented by bullies and hides out in an 
	unused janitor’s closet instead of going to class. When the boys are throw
	n together in detention\, they realize that they might still have a lot to
	 learn—not just from their teachers\, but from each other. Absent tells th
	e boys’ stories in three distinct voices: Manny’s in verse\, Calvin’s as a
	 comic\, and Liam’s as a prose journal. \n***Please note that the book sig
	ning will be conducted on a first-come\, first-served basis. Upon entering
	 the theater\, people wishing to have books signed will be given a number\
	, and the book signing will take place in numerical order after the on-sta
	ge presentation.*** \n\n\nhttps://inprinthouston.org/event/inprint-cool-br
	ains-series-presents-rex-ogle/\n\nFrom Inprint Houston
URL:https://inprinthouston.org/event/inprint-cool-brains-series-presents-re
	x-ogle/
LOCATION:Meyerland Performing and Visual Arts Middle School\, 10410 Manhatt
	an Drive\, Houston\, TX\, 77096\, United States
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:inprint-houston
CATEGORIES:Inprint Houston
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT2H
END:VEVENT
